00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.240
that connect all of
these points of presence

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.420
and our data centers
and various other pieces

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.570
that we provide for
you together to make

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.200
it one huge infrastructure
investment that delivers value

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.750
to all of you.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.500
And over the years,
as we've been building

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.380
these sub-sea cables and
all of these investments

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.040
have been made in
the cable world,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.400
have gotten us to
the point where

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.760
we have an extensive
team that's completely

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.050
focused on submarine cables.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.550
And I'm really,
really proud to talk

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.250
about the next slide, which
is something very unique

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.820
that we did this year.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.060
So, in January of
this year, we became

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.340
the first non-telco
company to announce

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.150
a completely private
sub-sea cable that

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.500
connects two continents.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.590
And this was the Curie sub-sea
cable, named after Marie Curie,

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.260
and it connects Los
Angeles to Chile.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.200
And I'm also very, very excited
to announce that-- well,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.780
not announce, but last week,
we announced the Dunant sub-sea

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:43.280
cable.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.510
This is again named after the
founder of the Red Cross, Andre

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.930
Dunant, and it connects the
east coast of the US to Europe.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.020
So this is the first
trans-Atlantic cable

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.450
that's actually, again,
owned completely privately

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:57.950
by a non-telco entity.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.380
So this just goes to
show some of these things

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.350
that we're trying to
do to bring more value

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.430
from our infrastructure to you.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.260
So just looking at
the numbers in terms

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.635
of what we've been doing with
respect to infrastructures,

00:05:10.635 --> 00:05:12.660
I'm going to point out
a few key numbers here.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.410
We've been spending
a lot of money

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.290
in terms of making this
infrastructure that underlies

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.360
our Google Cloud, and
over the past three years,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.840
we've spent over $30
billion in terms of expenses

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.730
to build out Google's
infrastructure.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.270
In addition, we've also
got twenty cloud regions

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.570
that we've announced.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.600
We have 125 points of
presence, which I talked about.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.080
We're going to go into
the dedicated interconnect

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.480
locations shortly as well,
but the really interesting

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.480
statistic here is the 100,000
miles of fiber optic cable

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.050
that we have.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.290
This is, if you look at all
of the fiber optic cables

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:47.260
that we've deployed across
the globe, it's 100,000 miles.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:48.880
That's, just to
put it in context,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.130
about a little bit over
a third of the distance

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:52.460
from here to the moon.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:55.960
So it's a lot of investment
in terms of these cables

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.630
and what I hope to
do is to tell you

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.090
a little bit about why
this adds value to you

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.340
and your deployments
running on Google Cloud.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.160
So all of this infrastructure
is the underlay

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.034
that lets us build our SDN
ecosystem on top of that,

00:06:10.034 --> 00:06:11.950
using things that you've
probably heard about,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.899
like Andromeda and
Espresso, and if you

00:06:14.899 --> 00:06:16.690
want to learn more
about this, I definitely

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.420
recommend that you go and attend
one of two sessions that we

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.880
have later in the
conference which

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.620
dive much deeper into this area
and about how it helps improve

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.764
performance of
different workloads

00:06:28.764 --> 00:06:31.180
that are running in the cloud,
and in terms of networking,

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.800
how it makes it so efficient.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.570
But if I were to point
out three key things here,

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.440
the first thing is, this lets
us provide this global network

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.240
and the construct
of the global VPC

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.030
that I'll talk about in a
minute, which basically takes

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.090
away a lot of your sprawl and
toil from managing a vast cloud

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.230
deployment.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.060
It gives you a global
reach by getting you

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.820
to a point very, very close
to where your customers are

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.700
and it also gives you various
benefits, like live migration.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.530
With live migration,
what we're able to do

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.690
is be able to keep
your VMs up when

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:02.560
we need to do something like,
say, a firmware update that

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.020
needs to be done on the
underlying infrastructure.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.860
And some of these things
really make it very easy

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.600
to deploy on Google
Cloud and help

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.304
your workloads keep running.

00:07:12.304 --> 00:07:13.720
And finally, because
of the way we

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.420
have all of this infrastructure
investment laid out

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.360
and we have this SDN
architecture built

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.270
on top of that, it also
helps things scale seamlessly

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:25.342
because every single piece
of our networking offering

00:07:25.342 --> 00:07:27.550
is built in such a way that
there are no chokepoints.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.770
Everything is built in
a distributed manner

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.050
to make it very, very
easy for you to scale.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.520
And we've continued to innovate
in this area with something

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.020
that we've announced
late last year,

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.100
which was Andromeda
2.1, which has actually

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:45.050
reduced our intra-zone latency
by 40% from Andromeda 2.0,

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:46.880
and if you look at
it overall, it's

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:51.020
about a 780% reduction in
terms of the latency going

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.240
from when we originally started
and introduced Andromeda.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:56.986
So we talked about
this infrastructure.

00:07:56.986 --> 00:07:58.360
Now let me go a
little bit deeper

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.940
into how this infrastructure
helps you build your cloud

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:01.610
deployment.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.130
And we're going to look
at some interesting things

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.050
we've done over the
past year and how

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:06.820
we help solve various
of those problems

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.590
that we talked about
in the beginning.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:11.320
So to begin with, I want
to talk about two pieces--

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.320
our Virtual Private Cloud
and our connectivity options.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:19.180
So when you look at Google
Cloud's VPC, the global VPC

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.030
that we provide, it's very,
very unique in the sense

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.420
that it provides you a
connectivity by default

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.630
across all of your
workloads that you

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:31.460
have running in Google Cloud, no
matter which region you're on.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.366
So what I have there is
a simple one sentence,

00:08:34.366 --> 00:08:35.740
where if I were
to describe this,

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.993
the way I would do that is
you may have 17 regions--

00:08:38.993 --> 00:08:41.409
you may have VMs running in
each and every one of these 17

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:42.159
regions--

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:44.710
but you have one VPC,
which means you have

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:46.330
to manage one set of policies.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:49.767
You have to manage
one set of security.

00:08:49.767 --> 00:08:51.850
You need to do all of your
security configurations

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:52.870
and everything once.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.240
And it really makes
it much easier

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.490
for you to manage your
truly global deployment.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:00.400
Now the other really
nice thing about this

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:04.000
is, since this is built in
this way, the only way we

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.340
are able to make something
like this work is because

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:09.725
of the infrastructure offerings
that we provide that gives you

00:09:09.725 --> 00:09:11.350
the kind of performance
that you really

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.580
need to pull something
off like this.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.250
Now let's look at what are
some different things that you

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.550
can do to connect to the
global VPC from your network.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.294
So we've had various
offerings, and this year, we

00:09:23.294 --> 00:09:25.710
talked about two key offerings,
which was the Google Cloud

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.234
Interconnect offerings.

00:09:27.234 --> 00:09:29.400
And when you look at Google
Cloud Interconnect, what

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.830
it does is these are
various offerings that

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.570
help you connect from
wherever you are,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.880
whether it's a data center
or whether you're looking

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.560
at a co-location facility.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.120
It gives you a
convenient way for you

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.941
to connect from where
you are to Google Cloud.

00:09:41.941 --> 00:09:43.440
And the way you
would do this is you

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.023
would connect to one of our many
dedicated Interconnect points

00:09:46.023 --> 00:09:47.690
of presence, and
once you do that, you

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.230
will be on the
Google Cloud network

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:50.377
for the rest of the way.

00:09:50.377 --> 00:09:52.710
The other interesting thing
I want to quickly touch upon

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.320
before diving deeper into
Google Cloud Interconnect is we

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:58.440
have also been
working on simplifying

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.820
all of your connectivity
options, including VPN.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.550
We've had a lot of
efforts that we've

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.805
invested in working
with our partners,

00:10:04.805 --> 00:10:06.180
and with our
partners, we've been

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.330
able to deliver to this whole
slew of integration guides

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.055
and making it extremely easy for
you to spin up any kind of VPN

00:10:12.055 --> 00:10:16.100
connectivity when you want
to connect to Google Cloud.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.530
So I mentioned about
Dedicated Interconnect.

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:20.810
So let me tell you
what we think makes

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.460
Dedicated Interconnect unique.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.819
So when you look at
a connectivity option

00:10:24.819 --> 00:10:26.360
where you need to
connect to a cloud,

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.430
let's imagine that you
have a data center here

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.997
in San Francisco and
you have workloads

00:10:30.997 --> 00:10:32.580
that are running in
different regions.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.700
Let's say you have one
running in Los Angeles,

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.280
you have one running
in Singapore,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.720
and you have one running
somewhere in Europe.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.230
So just pick any three
data centers of your choice

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.560
and imagine that you
have this deployed.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.750
Now if you were trying to
do a dedicated connectivity

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.540
from your data
center to the cloud,

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.650
to each one of these
regions, usually you

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.620
would have to deploy
one set of connections

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.390
to each and every
one of these regions.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.310
With Dedicated Interconnect,
what you are able to do

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.694
is you're actually able to
connect from your data center

00:11:00.694 --> 00:11:01.610
to the nearest region.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.026
So in this case, you would
just connect to the Los Angeles

00:11:04.026 --> 00:11:06.380
region, and from there, you
can ride Google's backbone

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.787
to get to whichever other
data center that you need to.

00:11:08.787 --> 00:11:10.370
The other really
nice thing about this

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:12.280
is it greatly
simplifies the sprawl

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.030
and toil that come
from trying to build

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.520
these kind of deployments with
multiple connections going

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.650
to all of these
different regions.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.810
So this map shows all of
our Dedicated Interconnect

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:24.590
locations.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.150
So no matter where
you are, there

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.340
are these various locations
where you need to get to

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.680
and we bring you onto
the Google Cloud network

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.570
right from those points.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:35.030
We also work with
various partners and each

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:39.500
and every one of these
partners helps us provide you

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.170
with a much more simplified way
of connecting to Google Cloud.

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:44.030
So for instance, if
you were to connect

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.650
to any one of
these partners, you

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:48.440
get the flexibility to choose
the bandwidth that you need,

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.450
and they will help you
connect from wherever

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.000
you are to Google Cloud.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.310
So we talked about
how we've been trying

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.170
to simplify your
connectivity options when

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.605
you're trying to go from
your on-premise to the cloud.

00:12:00.605 --> 00:12:01.980
Now let's look at
what we've been

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:05.220
doing in terms of helping you
deliver applications better.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.140
So when you talk about
application delivery,

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.349
these are a few offerings
that we usually talk about,

00:12:10.349 --> 00:12:12.181
and I'm going to spend
a few minutes talking

00:12:12.181 --> 00:12:14.670
about what we've done in each
of these over the past year.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:17.045
So first, let's take a look
at Google Cloud's Global Load

00:12:17.045 --> 00:12:17.890
Balancing.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:22.560
So we built Google Cloud's
Global Load Balancing based off

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.064
all of the lessons
that we learned over

00:12:25.064 --> 00:12:27.480
the years in terms of building
and delivering applications

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:31.050
like Gmail and Google
Search and YouTube and all

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.272
of these different
offerings, and you'll also

00:12:33.272 --> 00:12:34.980
notice that that's
actually true for each

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.521
and every one of
these things that we

00:12:36.521 --> 00:12:38.530
look at in the application
delivery portfolio.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.150
And when you see this, we
got a lot of great feedback

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.400
in terms of how the Google
Cloud Load Balancer works

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.316
and how it makes it
extremely simple to manage

00:12:46.316 --> 00:12:48.949
a scalable load
balancing deployment,

00:12:48.949 --> 00:12:51.240
and we've kind of tried to
take that over the last year

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.570
and go to the next step.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.780
So our Google Cloud
Load Balancer,

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.180
for those of you who aren't
familiar with it, supports

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.270
Global Anycast IP
load balancing,

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.980
which means rather than
having to use your own DNS

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.560
service to load balance
to multiple IP addresses

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.810
depending on where your
traffic is coming from,

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:10.680
you can use your load balancer
to use a single Global Anycast

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:14.140
IP so that if you have a user,
for instance, in this example

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.780
who is trying to get onto
the Google Cloud network

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:18.850
to reach your application
from San Francisco,

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.465
they would still be using the
same IP address, as you would

00:13:21.465 --> 00:13:24.090
see for a customer who's trying
to connect to your Google Cloud

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:25.650
deployment from Singapore,

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.100
but the services would
be provided by the data

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:29.740
center that's closest to them.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:33.060
So this does various things,
and the first and foremost

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:36.210
is it greatly simplifies what
you need to do on the DNS front

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:38.610
in terms of configuring
and managing a deployment

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:39.511
like this.

00:13:39.511 --> 00:13:41.010
Now the really cool
thing that we've

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.870
done in this area
in the past year

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.020
is we've enhanced a lot of
different protocol supports

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.210
that we've added over the
past year, and one of them

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:50.910
specifically that I want to call
out is our support for QUIC.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:54.270
So QUIC actually
is a protocol that

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:57.300
would help you gain a lot in
terms of your connectivity time

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.730
and in terms of the
latency that it actually

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.430
takes for your users to
get onto this application

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:03.430
that you're looking for.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:08.310
So for instance, we see
about an 8% decrease

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:10.192
in terms of page
load times globally,

00:14:10.192 --> 00:14:11.900
and especially if
you're in an area where

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:13.950
there is a lot of
latency or you're

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.160
in high latency environment,
it goes up much higher--

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.840
it's about 13% in
those kind of areas.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.270
So we do have a great
session about load balancing

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.936
that's happening later
in the conference

00:14:22.936 --> 00:14:25.200
as well, where we go much
deeper into this area

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.270
and many other things about
our load balancing offering.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:32.370
Google Cloud DNS is built to
work with our load balancer

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.320
so it works out
really, really well

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.890
in this particular deployment,
where it integrates seamlessly

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.590
with our Global Anycast
load balancing offering.

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.710
It also has a very, very simple
scalable record management

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.494
and it also supports
DNSSEC so that you

00:14:45.494 --> 00:14:47.160
can verify the integrity
of your records

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:51.410
when your clients are trying
to connect to your setup.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:54.270
Now let's look at Google Cloud's
Content Delivery Network.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:57.410
So like I mentioned, our
content delivery system as well

00:14:57.410 --> 00:14:59.240
is built off of a
lot of the things

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.670
that we've learned over the
years from YouTube and Google

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.440
Search and all of
our other offerings.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.120
And what we've been
able to do here

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:06.980
is to try and take
some of those lessons

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:10.700
and bring to you a very,
very scalable global approach

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:12.770
to delivering your
content across the globe.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:14.840
So one thing that we've
done over the past year

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:17.780
is we recently announced support
for very, very large-sized

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:21.720
objects, which makes it great
for media and applications

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.680
where you're trying to either
deliver media or gaming.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:28.160
So this is again something great
that we're very, very proud of,

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:29.675
and we do have a
session about CDN

00:15:29.675 --> 00:15:31.550
as well if you're
interested in learning more

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.740
about this offering.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.800
We announced network
tiers at Next last year.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:41.130
Network tiers is now in
beta and network tiers

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:45.450
is basically what makes
your global VPC work.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.670
So the network
tiers is an offering

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.400
that lets your traffic
travel Google's network

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.120
all the way through from
the source to your end user

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.740
until a point of presence
that's very, very close to them.

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.680
So by doing this, it's
able to provide you

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.930
with much better latency
and much better performance

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:03.005
than you would be
used to on a regular--

00:16:03.005 --> 00:16:05.260
when you're using
the public internet.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:09.420
So last year we heard from
you that-- and sometimes there

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:13.080
are certain work loads where
this extra performance is not

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.580
necessarily what
you are looking for

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:16.570
and you would look for more
cost-effective offerings,

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:18.600
so that's where we
introduced the standard tier,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.760
and the standard tier
is currently beta.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.160
And usually when you
look at customers

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.870
and when they use
premium network

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.940
versus standard network,
our customers really

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.630
like to use the premium network
for any live applications

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:34.290
or applications that are being
accessed by their end users,

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.790
whereas in a lot
of cases, they like

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.797
to use the standard network for
things like a test deployment

00:16:38.797 --> 00:16:40.380
where you're trying
to build something

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.280
and making changes in your
development environment.

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:43.920
So those kind of
environments usually

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:45.480
use the standard
network, as opposed

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.790
to the premium network for
the production environment.

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:51.600
Now we talked about
connecting to the cloud

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.210
and we talked about
delivering your applications

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:55.149
to your customers.

00:16:55.149 --> 00:16:56.940
Next, we're going to
look at various things

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:58.898
that we have been doing
in the area of securing

00:16:58.898 --> 00:16:59.800
your deployments.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:01.620
So we got a lot
of great feedback

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:04.290
in terms of how we've been
making more and more offerings

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:07.170
available in terms of
security for our users,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.079
and in this area, we focused
on a few specific things

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:12.720
to try and make those very, very
user-friendly and very, very

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:13.420
easy.

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:16.140
So the first one is
VPC Service Controls.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.240
So what VPC Service
Controls does

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.680
is it greatly mitigates any
kind of exfiltration risk

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:23.839
you may have by
providing you controls

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:26.400
which are context-aware
and let you

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.950
enforce policy for accessing
files not just based

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.930
on the identity of the user
but also based on the context.

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:34.600
So this gives you much more
visibility into your data,

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:36.352
which is the lifeblood
of your business,

00:17:36.352 --> 00:17:38.810
and it really helps you control
where this data is flowing.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.750
Network monitoring is one area
where we got a lot of feedback

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.616
from our customers, saying
that when you look at a cloud

00:17:48.616 --> 00:17:50.490
deployment and you look
at network monitoring

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:52.170
offerings that are
available there,

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.780
one big concern
for our customers

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:58.890
was that it doesn't map one
to one when you get started

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:02.174
in terms of what we have
with the level of visibility

00:18:02.174 --> 00:18:03.840
that you look at in
the cloud as opposed

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.173
to what you look at when
you're doing something on-prem.

00:18:06.173 --> 00:18:08.532
And so we took that
and we went back

00:18:08.532 --> 00:18:09.990
and we tried to
build this offering

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:12.030
and we announced
VPC Flow Logs, which

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.970
is now generally available,
and with VPC Flow Logs, what

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.475
you can do is you get
five-second interval updates.

00:18:17.475 --> 00:18:25.800
So this is almost as
responsive as what you

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:28.216
would see in your data centers.

00:18:28.216 --> 00:18:30.840
And the really nice thing about
doing something like this is it

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:32.591
lets you have the same
level of visibility

00:18:32.591 --> 00:18:34.923
that you would when you're
deploying things in your data

00:18:34.923 --> 00:18:36.690
center and it
integrates seamlessly

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.480
with all of your deployments
that you have on-prem as well,

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.750
because we partner with many
of our security partners

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.560
to provide visibility
into these logs.

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.140
So what you could
do is you could

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.645
export these logs to
your partner ecosystem

00:18:48.645 --> 00:18:51.020
that you're already using and
have a single pane of glass

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:55.050
where you can visualize both
your traffic in your data

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.880
center as well as what
you're using in the cloud.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.870
And what helps us make
this even more flexible

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.570
is that we have a very,
very rich set of parameters

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:08.110
that we provide to you in terms
of filtering this flow log.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.020
So we let you annotate
based on things

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:12.030
like the geolocation
of your client

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.670
who is trying to
access the service,

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.424
the region where
your VM is running,

00:19:16.424 --> 00:19:18.090
and various other
things like the subnet

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.660
and various other
things that let

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:21.960
you get the logs that
you're really looking for

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:26.250
and just use those in
order to keep a close eye

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.040
and get great visibility
into the security

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.937
posture of your deployment.

00:19:31.937 --> 00:19:34.270
The next thing we're going
to talk about is Cloud Armor.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:38.080
Cloud Armor is our denial
of service defense system

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:41.320
that we've built and we
announced in March this year,

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:44.110
and in fact, we have
a session later today

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:47.380
where Nick will also be
presenting about Target

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.990
and how they have been using
the Cloud Armor service as well.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:51.490
So there are a few
key things I want

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:53.050
to point out about Cloud Armor.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.420
So Cloud Armor is built
to integrate very,

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.220
very closely with
our load balancer,

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:00.165
so what tends to happen is
we are able to take away

00:20:00.165 --> 00:20:03.320
a brunt of any kind
of a layer three DDoS

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:06.400
attack that would try to
hit you at the load balancer

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:09.280
Then beyond that, we have
denial of service defense

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:12.550
that is built to protect
you against application

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:15.769
attacks and various
things like SQL injection

00:20:15.769 --> 00:20:16.810
and cross-site scripting.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.480
So Cloud Armor provides
you with a rules language

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:22.477
that lets you program
things beyond things

00:20:22.477 --> 00:20:24.310
like SQL injection and
cross-site scripting,

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:26.944
where you can
tailor your security

00:20:26.944 --> 00:20:28.360
policy to fit
exactly what you are

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.340
doing on your own deployment.

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:33.010
And the nice thing about
the way Cloud Armor is built

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:37.540
is that a lot of
this defense actually

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:40.480
happens at the
perimeter, which means

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:42.265
it makes it much easier
for you to manage

00:20:42.265 --> 00:20:44.140
the scale of your setup
while using something

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:44.848
like Cloud Armor.

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.400
So we talked about all of
these different offerings.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.597
We talked about our connectivity
offerings, the security

00:20:53.597 --> 00:20:55.680
that we try to provide to
you with your networking

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.020
deployments, how you can get
your applications delivered

00:20:58.020 --> 00:20:59.184
to your customer.

00:20:59.184 --> 00:21:00.600
So in this next
section, I'm going

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.190
to talk about the
workload of your choice.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:07.050
So we have been having more and
more customers adopt Kubernetes

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.720
and we have a lot of customers
who have been adopting GKE,

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:13.290
and they're really adopting
container technologies

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:14.520
and making them their own.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:16.124
So what we've been
focusing on is

00:21:16.124 --> 00:21:18.540
we've been trying to make
Google Cloud the cloud of choice

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:20.160
to run your micro-services.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.080
So we are trying to
provide native support

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.150
for every single networking
service that we have

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:27.072
and networking offering that
we have for Kuberetes Engine.

00:21:27.072 --> 00:21:29.030
Like, for instance, this
year we have support--

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:31.150
Kubernetes Engine supports
Global Load Balancing,

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:33.370
it supports our denial
of service product,

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:34.450
which is Cloud Armor.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:37.740
It supports IAP
and our CDN, and we

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.570
are committed to making
sure that every one

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:42.070
of our networking offerings
is available for you

00:21:42.070 --> 00:21:45.680
with Kubernetes Engine as well.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:47.180
And we've also
taken the next step

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.120
in terms of securing Kubernetes
workload so that you can look

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:53.720
at your security whether
you're looking at containers

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:55.910
or you're looking at compute.

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:57.510
You can look at them
in the same way.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:58.926
So we announced
support for things

00:21:58.926 --> 00:22:01.240
like shared VPC,
private clusters,

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:03.860
we support network policies,
distributed firewalls,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:04.892
VPC flow logging--

00:22:04.892 --> 00:22:06.350
all of these are
supported natively

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.162
now with Kubernetes, which
makes it extremely easy for you

00:22:09.162 --> 00:22:11.120
to manage your security
policies whether you're

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.330
doing Compute Engine or
Container Engine deployments.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.229
And if you're looking to
connect your different services,

00:22:18.229 --> 00:22:19.770
I'm sure you already
heard about some

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:21.420
of this in today
morning's keynote

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.856
and you're going to hear a lot
more over the next few days

00:22:23.856 --> 00:22:25.980
in terms of various sessions
where people are going

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:28.699
to talk to you about
Istio and Kubernetes

00:22:28.699 --> 00:22:30.240
and what they're
doing in these areas

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:33.300
to really make it easy to
use, manage, and secure.

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:36.870
And to this point,
we also have support

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:39.030
for all of these things
in different ways

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:42.940
in our networking offerings,
so like for instance,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.560
in our networking
team, we also have

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:48.360
the gRPC under our
portfolio, which

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:55.320
lets you connect basically your
services in terms of RPC calls

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:57.510
and making sure that it's
extremely easy for you

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:00.120
to build a service
without having

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.160
to put all the brunt off
the effort of building

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.410
and deploying the
networking aspects of this

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:05.560
on your developers.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.560
So what you can do is
you can use ecosystems

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:11.166
like Istio and gRPC and make
it available to your developers

00:23:11.166 --> 00:23:13.290
so that they can focus on
building the applications

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.546
without having to worry a
lot about the networking

00:23:15.546 --> 00:23:17.670
and securing the network
communication parts, which

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:20.820
can be taken care of, then, by
your security admins, who would

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.310
use tools like Istio
and gRPC to make sure

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:27.070
that this communication
is secured.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:30.640
We also have a lot of partners
in the areas of security

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.750
and we're working with adding
many, many more every day.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.410
We keep talking to
different partners

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:37.000
and we try to see
how we can integrate

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.880
with them to make a better
offering available to you

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:41.210
every single day.

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:44.380
And so this is just a quick
snapshot of various partners

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:45.942
that we have in
the security space,

00:23:45.942 --> 00:23:47.650
and I'm sure you
recognize a lot of names

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:49.340
there, so we do
work with them very,

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:52.060
very closely to make
Google Cloud a very, very

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:56.620
partner-friendly offering when
it comes to network security.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:58.450
So with that, I
just want to recap

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:01.120
by saying thank you so
much for all the feedback

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:02.630
that you gave us
in the last year

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:05.890
and it's really helped
us empower your success

00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:08.410
by trying to come
and make it easier

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:10.870
for you to move to the cloud
by meeting you where you are

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:13.000
and providing you these
secure and flexible

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:16.440
yet simple and reliable
offerings that can really

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:19.000
work for any workload that
you're looking to use.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.265
And with that, I
definitely want to also say

00:24:21.265 --> 00:24:23.390
I'm really looking forward
to talking to all of you

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:26.399
and hearing what you have in
mind for us for the next year

00:24:26.399 --> 00:24:28.690
and what you would like to
hear about when we come back

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.840
to Next 2019 next year.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:33.470
So now I'm going to
call Nick on stage

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:35.670
to come and talk about what
a lot of these offerings

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:38.741
meant from a
customer perspective.

00:24:38.741 --> 00:24:39.920
NICK JACQUES: Thank you.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:41.650
All right.

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:42.820
Hi, everyone.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:43.820
My name is Nick Jacques.

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:46.520
I am a lead engineer
at Target and I work

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.890
on our cloud platform team.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:51.440
I'm really excited to be here
today and chat with everyone

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:54.840
about some of the things
that we're doing in GCP.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.220
So just a little bit about
who I am and what Target is.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:01.760
So Target is a rather popular
retailer here in the US.

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:05.926
We have 1,800 stores,
several distribution centers,

00:25:05.926 --> 00:25:07.550
a few headquarters,
locations, and then

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:11.630
we also have one very big
virtual store, Target.com.

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:14.390
I've been working on our cloud
platform team for about 2

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:17.109
and 1/2, 3 years now,
and prior to that,

00:25:17.109 --> 00:25:19.400
I was on a couple of our
different infrastructure teams

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:22.070
dealing with enterprise
storage and virtualization,

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:25.019
private cloud build-out, all
kinds of good stuff like that.

00:25:25.019 --> 00:25:27.560
The reason why we thought it'd
be really appropriate to share

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:31.730
some of this with you is Target
started its journey in GCP

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:33.130
in early 2017.

00:25:33.130 --> 00:25:34.640
So it's been a
little over a year

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:38.732
since we really have
migrated to GCP and Earnest,

00:25:38.732 --> 00:25:40.190
so we thought that
kind of lined up

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:43.940
with the discussion about what's
new over the past year in GCP.

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.340
Just to give you a little
time table, in very early

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.950
2017 is when we started
migrating our workloads

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:51.710
from another cloud
provider into GCP,

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:53.390
and we were able
to complete that I

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:56.000
want to say around
late July, mid-August,

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:57.950
so right in time
for peak season.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.680
We had a very successful
peak season with no issues

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:03.320
and everyone was really
happy about that.

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:05.750
Early this year,
we had discussions

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:08.340
around where we were going to
migrate our commerce workloads.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:10.550
So we kind of split these
into two categories.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:13.690
What non-commerce means for us
is, when you're on Target.com

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.160
or if you're using
one of our apps,

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:17.900
it's basically
anything that doesn't

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:19.710
involve purchasing products.

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:22.160
So if you're searching
for products,

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:25.256
if you're swiping through
looking at images of products,

00:26:25.256 --> 00:26:27.380
things like that, that's
what we call non-commerce.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:29.796
And then when we actually have
you add items to your cart,

00:26:29.796 --> 00:26:31.850
check out, and then
pay for those items,

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:33.650
those are kind of
the commerce things.

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.233
Those tend to be a little more
difficult to do because there's

00:26:36.233 --> 00:26:38.930
quite a bit of regulatory
compliance involved with them.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.560
So we started migrating our
commerce workloads in Earnest

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:44.930
right around the middle
of this year, probably

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:48.907
around March or April, and
actually, we're almost 100%

00:26:48.907 --> 00:26:50.990
complete with that so we're
pretty excited that we

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:55.580
have almost all the things that
power Target.com in GCP right

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:56.610
now.

00:26:56.610 --> 00:27:00.410
So just to give you kind of
the gamut of what's in there,

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:02.360
we have quite a
bit that's there.

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:05.240
A lot of these services that
I list under Target.com are

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:07.940
actually shared and our
mobile apps use those services

00:27:07.940 --> 00:27:11.630
to display content as well,
but we have everything from

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:15.020
our adaptive frontend-- so
basically giving you responsive

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:17.600
design depending on what
you're using-- desktop, laptop,

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.060
tablets, mobile devices,
things like that--

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:21.365
and also our backends.

00:27:21.365 --> 00:27:23.359
Our backends kind
of spread the gamut

00:27:23.359 --> 00:27:25.400
between things that are
very monolithic-- they're

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:27.980
kind of standalone
apps-- to some very

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:31.100
complicated applications
that have upwards of three

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:33.500
or four dozen micro-services.

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:36.260
We also operate a
data persistence layer

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:39.440
and provide replication services
to and from our data center

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:42.300
and a platform for
logging in metrics.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:43.670
These last three items--

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:45.500
the data persistence,
replication,

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:46.790
and logging in metrics--

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:49.700
are part of what we refer
to as platform services

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:52.430
and that's part of what my team
helps offer our application

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:54.800
teams or our tenants in GCP.

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:58.520
We provide a suite of services
that any application team

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:00.920
can use and basically
provides common functionality

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:02.570
around any cloud
that we deploy to.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.790
So it abstracts a lot
of the difficulties

00:28:04.790 --> 00:28:07.640
away from our application
teams in terms of figuring out

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:10.340
how to do service discovery,
where to store data,

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:12.120
how to replicate data,
things like that,

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:14.267
and really lets them focus
on building their apps

00:28:14.267 --> 00:28:16.100
and then deploying those
apps and not really

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:18.866
having to worry about some of
that underlying infrastructure.

00:28:18.866 --> 00:28:20.240
And then, as I
mentioned, we also

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:23.030
operate some of our
commerce stuff in GCP,

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:27.530
and so those things are PCI
DSS compliant, which obviously

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:30.230
means that there is a great
deal of regulatory compliance

00:28:30.230 --> 00:28:32.270
that's attached to it,
and those environments

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.190
are highly segmented.

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.292
For mobile apps-- so in
addition to everything

00:28:36.292 --> 00:28:38.000
that we just talked
about for Target.com,

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.370
we also have some
unique mobile offerings,

00:28:40.370 --> 00:28:41.750
so we have things
like Cartwheel,

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:43.250
which is part of
our mobile app that

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:46.100
lets you discover coupons
and various discounts that

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:47.790
are available in the store.

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:49.770
Wallet, which is
actually really great.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:52.880
It allows you to take all those
coupons and discounts that you

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.640
discover with Cartwheel
and pay with your red card

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:58.550
in a single scan of a
barcode so it's really handy.

00:28:58.550 --> 00:28:59.467
I use it all the time.

00:28:59.467 --> 00:29:01.841
And then we also provide some
other interesting services,

00:29:01.841 --> 00:29:02.770
like in-store mapping.

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:04.880
So we can tell you where
a particular product is

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:06.380
that you're searching
for in a store

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:08.421
and then actually show
where you are in the store

00:29:08.421 --> 00:29:10.890
and you can kind of find
your way to that product.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.080
There are a few other
mobile apps as well.

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:16.250
And then we also host a
variety of API endpoints.

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:19.910
So we actually host our
developer documentation

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:23.300
at developer.target.com on
GCP, as well as a variety

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:25.950
of API backends in GCP.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.750
So these are everything
from item availability,

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:30.800
creating shipping labels,
creating barcodes,

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.430
things like that.

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:35.310
We also host a variety
of static content.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:37.760
So think images,
JavaScript files,

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:40.290
all kinds of stuff like
that, in GCS buckets,

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.200
and we also use Cloud DNS.

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:47.130
So I sat for a while and
tried to think about,

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:51.210
how can I best communicate
all the various features

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.775
that we use in terms of
networking and infrastructure

00:29:53.775 --> 00:29:55.230
in GCP?

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:58.320
And I thought for a while
about this and ultimately

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.842
what I decided to do is turn
to a tool that a lot of people

00:30:00.842 --> 00:30:03.300
use in large companies when
they're making decisions, which

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:07.050
is a particular geometric
shape that tends to be or have

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.467
magical properties.

00:30:08.467 --> 00:30:10.050
So I looked at this--
and I definitely

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:11.216
didn't make this on a plane.

00:30:11.216 --> 00:30:12.240
This is very official.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:15.590
And what I saw was that word
cloud, for the eighth year,

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:19.470
is dominating the information
density representation.

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.320
So with that in mind, here
are some of the features

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:25.590
that we use in terms of
networking and infrastructure

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:26.949
for Target.

00:30:26.949 --> 00:30:28.240
So there's quite a few up here.

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.865
I'm not going to go through them
all now because we'll actually

00:30:30.865 --> 00:30:33.390
kind of just naturally
go through those as we

00:30:33.390 --> 00:30:35.020
work through these slides.

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.230
So briefly what I
want to talk about

00:30:37.230 --> 00:30:39.370
are the environments
that we have.

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:42.450
So as I mentioned, our
non-commerce environment

00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:46.710
has to do a lot with basically
browsing and discovering

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:49.440
products and services
on Target.com.

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:53.290
So the way those are structured,
we have several environments.

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:57.300
So we have a dev environment,
a non-prod for staging

00:30:57.300 --> 00:30:59.370
and performance
purposes environment,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:01.120
and then we have a
production environment.

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:02.745
And you can think of
those environments

00:31:02.745 --> 00:31:06.240
basically as single
monolithic projects.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:08.610
Inside of those projects,
there is a single VPC

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:11.930
that contains many
different regional subnets,

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:14.550
and what we've done is something
kind of interesting here.

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.290
One of the challenges
we've previously

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:18.750
encountered in prior
cloud providers

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.210
and just due to the
nature of Target

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:23.700
itself is IP address exhaustion.

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:26.040
So particularly in
a cloud, you want

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:28.380
to be able to scale and
not run into any issues

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:31.390
where you've exhausted a
subnet and now what do you do.

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:32.820
So to get around
those challenges,

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.290
because these
tenants are actually

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.750
quite large in size and
scale through many thousands

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:39.740
of instances, is we
actually kind of divorced

00:31:39.740 --> 00:31:43.410
the application subnets from
our data center routeability.

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.480
So what that means is
all of our applications

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:48.180
that operate in our non-commerce
environment are basically

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:50.790
air-gapped from the data
center, but what that means

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:54.150
for us is we've created several
very large subnets so we don't

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:58.230
have to worry about partitioning
off specific applications

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:00.484
and specific subnets
or things like that.

00:32:00.484 --> 00:32:02.400
It's just a very large
general-purpose hosting

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:04.020
environment.

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:06.720
The way that we allow
the applications to talk

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.660
to services in our data
center or replicate data

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:13.740
across is that platform
services suite of components

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.160
that I mentioned earlier.

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.430
Those services are data
center routeable and so

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:20.010
that's how we replicate
that data across,

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:22.720
and then the application
teams can consume it in GCP.

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.900
If we take a look, then, at
our commerce environment,

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.320
that environment is
actually quite different.

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:34.020
Yes, it's still hosted
in GCP, and yes, we still

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:35.520
have our platform
services there,

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:37.930
but many things beyond
that are different.

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:40.010
So, for compliance
purposes, we wanted

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.840
to make sure that we had a
highly segmented environment

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:43.510
here.

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:46.300
So what we've done is we've
actually created many projects

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:49.350
and we've used shared
VPC and VPC peering

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:51.600
to give us that really
highly segmented environment

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:54.690
and help us partition off each
individual application that

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:56.562
runs there.

00:32:56.562 --> 00:32:58.020
In this model, all
of our instances

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.180
are data center routeable,
and we made that choice

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:02.340
because this
environment is actually

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:04.170
at least an order
of magnitude smaller

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.180
than our non-commerce
environment,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.600
and many of those applications
have direct requirements

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:12.360
for synchronous or asynchronous
calls to our data center

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:14.805
to complete things like
payment transactions and so on.

00:33:14.805 --> 00:33:16.680
So we needed to give
those applications a way

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:19.140
to directly hook
into the data center.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:22.470
As I mentioned, these
tenants are highly segmented

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:24.960
and basically everything
in this environment

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.600
is subject to
regulatory compliance.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:30.062
So obviously we wanted to make
sure that we got it right.

00:33:30.062 --> 00:33:31.770
Going through some of
the common patterns

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:34.470
that we have across both
of these environments,

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:38.010
we currently use IPsec VPNs
to connect our data centers

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:39.924
to these particular
environments.

00:33:39.924 --> 00:33:41.590
One of the things
that we're looking at,

00:33:41.590 --> 00:33:45.256
which was mentioned just a bit
ago, is partner interconnect,

00:33:45.256 --> 00:33:47.130
and that's something
that we're investigating

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:48.870
and we'll be taking
a deeper look at that

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:52.050
either later this
year or early 2019.

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:55.530
We also use Cloud DNS
basically across all

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:57.570
of our environments
in GCP and that

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:01.950
hosts the authoritative DNS
for our internal resources

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:04.290
as well as our
external resources.

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:05.790
Our developers are
a really big fan

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:07.290
of this because
this means that they

00:34:07.290 --> 00:34:12.360
can use a common consolidated
Terraform repository

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:14.326
to create DNS entries
for their applications

00:34:14.326 --> 00:34:16.409
and they don't need to cut
tickets to another team

00:34:16.409 --> 00:34:18.591
to create DNS entries
and wait for another team

00:34:18.591 --> 00:34:19.590
to complete those tasks.

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:23.100
So we really empower developers
by letting them create

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:25.469
their DNS entries themselves.

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:27.960
For any type of
situation where instances

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:29.940
need to reach out
to the internet,

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:32.280
and this is actually
fairly common given

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:36.030
that API.Target.com is
surfaced on the internet,

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:40.179
we provide either a
NAT or a proxy service.

00:34:40.179 --> 00:34:41.940
So these services
are instrumented

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:45.570
with packet capturing
software and they basically

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:47.850
allow us to police
traffic that's

00:34:47.850 --> 00:34:50.364
egressing our caught environment
and take a look at what's

00:34:50.364 --> 00:34:52.739
going on there so that we can
react to it should anything

00:34:52.739 --> 00:34:54.420
happen.

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:56.270
As I mentioned
several times already,

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.390
our platform services
are consistent across all

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:00.520
of these environments.

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:06.240
We also use the HTTPS load
balancers almost universally.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.430
I would say that
there are probably

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:12.390
six load balancers that are
not the L7 load balancers.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.000
We love them.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.100
They're great.

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:15.780
They give us an
Anycast IP address,

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:18.194
we operate services
in multiple regions,

00:35:18.194 --> 00:35:20.610
and all we do is wire those
services up with the same load

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.990
balancer and away we go.

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:25.170
We don't have to worry about, is
this IP address in this region

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:27.189
and how do we
geolocate our users

00:35:27.189 --> 00:35:29.730
and make sure that they're going
to the region close to them.

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:32.460
It's all handled through
that load balancer.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:35.310
The way that we let our
application teams manage

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:38.030
their deployments and manage
the instances they're running on

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:40.950
is through an open-source
tool called Spinnaker.

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:43.200
It's a really great
tool and I would highly

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.210
encourage you to
check it out if you're

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:49.080
able to use it in your
particular environment.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:52.800
It really takes away a lot
of the pain of creating

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:54.630
a deployment pattern.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:56.790
You don't have to worry
about having people

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:59.580
login to an instance and run
scripts or anything like that.

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:04.620
Basically, you create some sort
of package install deliverable,

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.400
Spinnaker will bake that into
an instance template for you,

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:09.900
and then you can deploy that
as many times as you would like

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:11.640
and deploy it in
multiple instance groups

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:13.723
and it makes things really
easy, so I would highly

00:36:13.723 --> 00:36:15.840
recommend that.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.090
One of the things that
we use fairly heavily

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.460
is private IP Google access.

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:23.520
The reason why we use
this heavily is almost

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:26.400
every instance in
GCP that we operate

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:29.590
does not have a public
or external IP address.

00:36:29.590 --> 00:36:32.025
Our NATs and proxies are
there for that purpose,

00:36:32.025 --> 00:36:34.150
and so what we want to do
is keep all that traffic,

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:37.200
especially traffic
that's destined to APIs,

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:40.800
for example, to GCS, just
internal to our VPCs.

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:43.740
So we use private IP
Google access for that.

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:46.440
We collect and analyze
logs across all

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:47.550
of these environments.

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:50.580
And then finally, we use
Cloud Armor and SSL policies

00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:53.460
to make sure that our
load balancers at the edge

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.380
match our security posture
that we'd like them to have.

00:36:56.380 --> 00:37:00.420
So for instance, only accepting
TLS version 1.2 connections,

00:37:00.420 --> 00:37:01.840
things like that.

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:04.340
So we've talked
through a lot of lists.

00:37:04.340 --> 00:37:05.940
What I want to do
is give you a visual

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.080
that we can take a look at.

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:08.850
So at a very high
level, this is what

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.260
our non-commerce
environment looks like.

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:13.020
So what we have
in our application

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:15.330
hosting area that's not
really pictured here

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:18.810
are several subnets that
span multiple regions.

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:22.140
But roughly what happens
is we have a Layer 7 load

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.780
balancer that fronts
our various endpoints.

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:29.099
So it could be an application
with multiple URL routes.

00:37:29.099 --> 00:37:30.640
It could be just a
single-purpose app

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:32.380
or a micro-service.

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:34.930
And then we have that partition
for platform services again.

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:36.888
So again, there's things
like service discovery

00:37:36.888 --> 00:37:40.480
that we host in there, data
persistence, data replication,

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:42.250
things like that.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:45.959
Then, as pictured, we have a VPN
that connects this environment

00:37:45.959 --> 00:37:47.500
to our data center,
and you'll notice

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:50.710
that we've attached only the
platform services subnets

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:52.110
to our data center.

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:54.550
The application
hosting subnets really

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:57.442
only talk to our platform
services and that's about it.

00:37:57.442 --> 00:37:59.650
Then finally, the part that
we haven't touched on yet

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:02.410
is, what do we do with
requests that are inbound?

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:03.940
How do we actually
service requests

00:38:03.940 --> 00:38:07.540
from either our guests or API
calls or things like that?

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:09.772
How this works is we
have our clients--

00:38:09.772 --> 00:38:11.980
they might be our guests,
they might be a third party

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.450
that's using our APIs--

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:16.030
and the first step is
they'll hit our CDN provider.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:19.240
So our CDN provider is
effectively our edge.

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:22.930
And through there, in a variety
of caching and web application

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:26.442
firewall layers, eventually
we'll traverse back into GCP

00:38:26.442 --> 00:38:28.150
and we'll hit one of
these load balancers

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:30.860
that represents a particular
service that we're hosting.

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:33.590
So that's kind of things for
our non-commerce environment

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:34.660
in a nutshell.

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:36.689
If we go over to our
commerce environment,

00:38:36.689 --> 00:38:38.980
pay really close attention
because the picture is going

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:41.230
to look fairly
similar, but what you

00:38:41.230 --> 00:38:45.100
notice is we have a lot
more boxes down here

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:46.580
on the left-hand side.

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:49.810
So what we've done is we've used
shared VPC and what we've done

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:52.450
is we've partitioned out
each of these applications

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:54.359
into its own service project.

00:38:54.359 --> 00:38:55.900
So what that means
is our application

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:58.600
teams, through Spinnaker, have
full reign in their service

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.152
project to do deployments
that they would need to do

00:39:01.152 --> 00:39:02.860
and they can look at
all of the logs that

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:04.510
are collected via Stackdriver.

00:39:04.510 --> 00:39:07.010
This partitioning puts
us in a really good place

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:09.250
wherein application
teams can't look

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:10.760
at other app teams logs, right?

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:12.176
You can only see
the logs that are

00:39:12.176 --> 00:39:15.440
relevant to the workloads that
are operating in your project.

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:18.250
So that's been working
out very nicely for us.

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:20.740
We've done the exact same thing
with our platform services

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:23.460
as well, so no one is
immune to the segmentation

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:25.070
and partitioning.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:26.830
So each of our
platform services teams

00:39:26.830 --> 00:39:29.260
that operates each one of
these particular services

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:32.570
that we provide has their
own service project as well.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:35.890
And then what we've done and
the great part about shared VPC

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:38.530
is we consolidated all
of our network policy

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:40.210
into the host project.

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:43.660
So what that means is all of
our custom routes are there,

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:45.640
our VPN connections
are there but apply

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:48.470
to all of these service
projects as well,

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:51.570
and then our firewall
rules are there as well.

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:54.070
So the great thing about this
is we have a centralized place

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:56.350
for firewall rules, we don't
need to go hunting around

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:59.440
to see if a particular
application team has changed

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:01.780
a firewall rule, and in
fact, the application teams

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:04.510
can't change a firewall
rule because the service

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:06.380
projects won't allow it.

00:40:06.380 --> 00:40:08.830
So all of those firewall
rules are hosted and kind

00:40:08.830 --> 00:40:10.390
of consolidated in
the host project

00:40:10.390 --> 00:40:14.140
and then we provide our
developers again a Terraform

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:17.680
repository through which
they can enact those changes.

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:21.100
In this particular case,
they are heavily reviewed

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.610
and we ensure the safety before
we put those into effect.

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:26.720
And then the rest of
it is very similar.

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:28.990
We have VPNs that connect
us to the data center.

00:40:28.990 --> 00:40:32.770
In this case, on this GCP side,
anything can traverse the VPN

00:40:32.770 --> 00:40:34.720
and come back to the
Target data center.

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:36.520
And then we have a
firewall on our side

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:38.561
to make sure that only
the appropriate traffic is

00:40:38.561 --> 00:40:40.780
coming back or going to GCP.

00:40:40.780 --> 00:40:43.756
And then our internet path
over on the upper right

00:40:43.756 --> 00:40:45.380
is basically the same
as non-commerce--

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:48.130
we still use our CDN to direct
the traffic inbound for us.

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:52.020
OK.

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:53.910
So this was a really high level.

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:57.600
What I want to do now is kind
of zoom in to just the GCP

00:40:57.600 --> 00:40:59.670
area a little bit
and show you what's

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:02.380
going on there in a
little more detail.

00:41:02.380 --> 00:41:04.310
So the first thing
we'll take a look at,

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:06.870
we sometimes jokingly refer
to this as a load balancer

00:41:06.870 --> 00:41:09.300
sandwich, but for our
application teams, what

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:11.700
we do is we obviously
have the L7 load

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:14.279
balancer at the edge
for public ingress,

00:41:14.279 --> 00:41:16.320
and then we actually have
internal load balancers

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:19.920
that we use for connectivity
either inside the VPC

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:23.392
or from our data
center up into GCP.

00:41:23.392 --> 00:41:25.350
Below, what you'll see
in the Platform Services

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:27.330
section is things
got a lot bigger.

00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:28.830
We talked about a
lot of these items

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:30.996
already so I'll just gloss
over this really quickly,

00:41:30.996 --> 00:41:34.080
but a lot of these services
are common to each environment.

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.594
And as I mentioned, we
have proxy and NAT services

00:41:36.594 --> 00:41:38.010
available for these
workloads that

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:39.690
need to reach out to
the internet, where

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:41.581
we do police that egress.

00:41:41.581 --> 00:41:43.080
If we take a look
in the lower right

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:46.650
and look at the Network
section, there are some things

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:48.810
that we use that are kind
of inherent across all

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:50.410
of these environments.

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:53.400
So we use Cloud VPN and
Cloud Router together.

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:55.350
And what we actually
do in these scenarios

00:41:55.350 --> 00:41:58.530
is we use BGP to announce
the routes across.

00:41:58.530 --> 00:41:59.756
So this is really great.

00:41:59.756 --> 00:42:01.380
This saves us a lot
of time from having

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:04.352
to try to figure out how we
announce these static routes

00:42:04.352 --> 00:42:06.060
and putting all these
change requests in.

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:08.393
It makes things really easy
for us and it makes failover

00:42:08.393 --> 00:42:10.050
really easy for us as well.

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:12.474
We have Cloud firewall
rules, as I mentioned,

00:42:12.474 --> 00:42:14.640
which are inherent across
all of these environments,

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:16.560
and there are many
of them, and then

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:18.570
we use custom
routes to do things

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:22.980
like pull traffic destined to
the internet through our NATs.

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:26.250
So there really isn't a
good way for applications

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:31.015
to egress out any other
way than through our NAT.

00:42:31.015 --> 00:42:32.640
In the upper right--
we'll kind of just

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:35.500
gloss over these a little
bit, but we use many other GCP

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:36.000
offerings.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:37.950
We can see a sampling up there.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:40.440
So we use IM very heavily.

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:42.870
We use logging in Cloud
Pub/Sub, which has actually

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:44.290
been very helpful to us.

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:46.800
So what we do is we
have logging syncs

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:48.900
on a lot of our
Stackdriver topics

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:51.210
and we pull that into
Pub/Sub, and then

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:54.340
what we do at our data center
is subscribe to that topic

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:55.200
and ingest that.

00:42:55.200 --> 00:43:00.330
So instead of clouding up or
filling up our VPN tunnels

00:43:00.330 --> 00:43:04.050
to and from GCP, we basically
transmit that out of band

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:06.460
and pull it into our data
center, where it's logged,

00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:07.807
stored, and analyzed.

00:43:07.807 --> 00:43:10.140
And then finally, the last
piece that I want to touch on

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:12.270
are the Cloud Armor
and SSL policies

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:14.190
that we apply to all
of our load balancers

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.490
at the edge to make sure that
we have consistency in the way

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:21.040
that those endpoints
are accessed.

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:23.790
So just quickly going through
some highlights, things

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:26.500
that have been really great
for us over the past year,

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:29.010
the global L7 load
balancers, as I said earlier,

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:30.450
have been really great.

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:33.750
We're big fans of it and
I don't think we really

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:36.600
have any complaints
about it whatsoever.

00:43:36.600 --> 00:43:39.084
Inter-region networking
has been great for us.

00:43:39.084 --> 00:43:40.500
So this was something
that we were

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:44.460
able to adopt when
we moved to GCP

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.740
and it's basically built in.

00:43:46.740 --> 00:43:47.580
It's really quick.

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:48.901
It's really easy.

00:43:48.901 --> 00:43:50.650
We have absolutely no
complaints about it,

00:43:50.650 --> 00:43:52.360
and for some of our services--

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:54.900
so for example, a lot
of our data persistence

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:56.790
is stored in Cassandra.

00:43:56.790 --> 00:43:58.500
We can just replicate
across regions

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:01.430
without having to worry
about regional VPCs

00:44:01.430 --> 00:44:03.840
and peering those VPCs
together, or even going

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:06.180
to the level of running
VPNs in those VPCs

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:08.100
and then connecting
VPNs together

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:09.370
to get traffic across them.

00:44:09.370 --> 00:44:11.320
So that's been great.

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:14.760
Our IPsec VPNs have
been absolutely stable

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:17.424
and we've had a really
great success with those.

00:44:17.424 --> 00:44:18.840
The only reason
that we're looking

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:22.290
at moving to Interconnect
is we need a little more

00:44:22.290 --> 00:44:26.070
bandwidth than what the VPNs
can currently provide us with.

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:27.690
Cloud Armor has been fantastic.

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:30.630
That has been in place in
our commerce environment

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:33.000
since day one and
we're currently

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:35.430
finishing a rollout in our
non-commerce environment

00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:36.900
as well.

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:39.240
A little tidbit that
isn't talked about very

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:44.920
often is the NTP server that is
built into the metadata server.

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:45.630
So this is great.

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:49.440
What it does is it gives you
access to Google's NTP servers,

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:51.570
and you actually get
the time smearing

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:54.930
that Google provides with their
public time.google.com server

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:56.052
as well.

00:44:56.052 --> 00:44:57.510
So the great thing
here is we don't

00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:58.839
have to configure anything.

00:44:58.839 --> 00:45:00.630
This is all kind of
baked in and good to go

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:03.660
and we don't have to egress out
to the internet for NTP sync.

00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:05.250
So that's been really handy.

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:07.860
Private IP Google access,
I touched on that already.

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:10.240
That's been really great for us.

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:12.600
Another really great feature
that thankfully we've

00:45:12.600 --> 00:45:15.240
only run into one
time is the ability

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:18.000
to expand your subnets live.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:20.910
I think a lot of folks know
that no matter how you plan

00:45:20.910 --> 00:45:22.650
and how many
whiteboards you draw on

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:24.630
and how many spreadsheets
you fill out,

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:28.350
trying to set up your IP
space for cloud deployment,

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:30.180
something unexpected
always happens.

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:33.390
And in those cases, what's
actually really easy to do

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:36.090
in Google is if you haven't
consumed the space that

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:40.230
you'll be expanding to, you
can flip that /24 to a 23

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:42.939
or beyond, as long as
that space is available,

00:45:42.939 --> 00:45:43.980
and you can do that live.

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:47.010
So it's a really good
way if the unexpected

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:50.914
has happened for you to get a
little headroom for yourself.

00:45:50.914 --> 00:45:52.080
And then finally, Cloud DNS.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:55.170
Cloud DNS has been
great for us, and our,

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:56.700
as I mentioned
earlier, developers

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:59.310
love it to kind of
be in control of DNS

00:45:59.310 --> 00:46:01.860
and do that in a
self-service fashion.

00:46:01.860 --> 00:46:05.580
So then I want to just wrap up
with our lessons learned here.

00:46:05.580 --> 00:46:08.010
One of the biggest ones
is that everyone should

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:10.650
have and exercise DR plans.

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:14.190
We have an internal tool that
we use that basically allows

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:17.580
us to go through all of
our L7 load balancers

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:20.220
and disable traffic
to a given region.

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:22.080
We use this in
emergency situations

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:23.700
or when we have
issues with a stack

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:26.160
in those particular regions,
and for the most part,

00:46:26.160 --> 00:46:27.780
it's been working really well.

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:29.700
Typically within
about 15 or 20 minutes

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:31.260
after an incident
is called, we're

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:34.830
able to drain all of the ingress
traffic to a particular region

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:37.072
away and send that
traffic to other regions,

00:46:37.072 --> 00:46:39.030
and we just do that with
a simple config change

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:41.370
the load balancers so that's
absolutely great that we're

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:44.140
able to respond in that way.

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:46.560
If you're operating a hybrid
cloud, lots of planning

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:48.300
involved and lots
of communicating

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:50.250
with teams across
the organization.

00:46:50.250 --> 00:46:53.554
We've been very lucky to
have really great partner

00:46:53.554 --> 00:46:55.970
teams at Target, all the way
from our infrastructure teams

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:59.275
that help us set up the VPNs
through our platform services

00:46:59.275 --> 00:47:01.650
teams that we've partnered
with for that data replication

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:04.609
and storage, all the way
up through our app teams.

00:47:04.609 --> 00:47:06.150
And one of the things
that we've done

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:08.700
that's been really critical is
sat down with our application

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:10.991
teams and made sure that, as
we're planning things out,

00:47:10.991 --> 00:47:14.730
they're included and we take
their feedback into account

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:17.850
as we're building out these
new features for them.

00:47:17.850 --> 00:47:21.090
One interesting item is,
be careful of attaching

00:47:21.090 --> 00:47:23.700
the same backend service
to multiple load balancers.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:27.030
We encountered this last
year when we had teams

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:29.610
that, when they're operating
in that load balancer sandwich

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:31.950
model, created a
single backend service

00:47:31.950 --> 00:47:33.090
and attached it to both.

00:47:33.090 --> 00:47:36.240
And what happened in that
particular scenario is

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:37.809
the signals from
the load balancers

00:47:37.809 --> 00:47:39.600
were a little cross-wired
and they were not

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:43.920
able to scale that service based
on RPS from the Layer 7 load

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:44.550
balancer.

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:48.360
So if scaling based on
RPS is important to you,

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:50.130
avoid attaching
your backend service

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:53.160
to multiple load balancers.

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:54.780
One other interesting
piece that has

00:47:54.780 --> 00:47:56.520
been a little bit
of an adaptation

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:58.920
for our application teams
and our security teams

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:02.670
is that, with the exception of
Cloud Armor or the network load

00:48:02.670 --> 00:48:05.610
balancer, the firewall
rules that you

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:08.169
apply are applied to
the instance itself.

00:48:08.169 --> 00:48:10.710
I'm sorry, this actually does
apply to network load balancer.

00:48:10.710 --> 00:48:13.980
So for ILBs, for instance,
there is no firewall rule

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:15.810
that you attach
to the ILB, right?

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:17.410
It's the firewall
rule that you attach

00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:19.800
to the instance
that actually allows

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:21.200
that traffic through the ILB.

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:24.170
So a little bit of
working to get that

00:48:24.170 --> 00:48:26.510
communicated across
all of our teams.

00:48:26.510 --> 00:48:29.760
And then finally,
just be wary no matter

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:31.760
what you're doing-- if
they're static or dynamic

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:35.330
announcements-- of what routes
you announce back on premise.

00:48:35.330 --> 00:48:38.870
We're lucky to have BGP
route filters in place

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:41.240
on the Target side
of things, but we

00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:43.220
actually for a period
of time did wind up

00:48:43.220 --> 00:48:48.260
announcing what is actually our
internal DMZ space in our data

00:48:48.260 --> 00:48:51.210
centers that we
use in GCP as well.

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:53.570
And that's part of
that kind of separation

00:48:53.570 --> 00:48:55.730
that we operate our
non-commerce applications in.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:58.100
So luckily we had those
filters in place and nothing

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:01.016
bad happened but
just be wary of that.

00:49:01.016 --> 00:49:02.390
So I think that
about wraps it up

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:03.830
for me and I'll
turn it back over.

00:49:03.830 --> 00:49:04.940
Oh, sorry.

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:07.550
So kind of tying in with
a couple of other things,

00:49:07.550 --> 00:49:09.710
I'm sure you'll be hearing
plenty of announcements

00:49:09.710 --> 00:49:11.168
through the rest
of this conference

00:49:11.168 --> 00:49:12.380
and the rest of this year.

00:49:12.380 --> 00:49:14.810
Some things that Target
is looking forward to,

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:17.720
just a variety of enhancements
up here on the screen.

00:49:17.720 --> 00:49:20.690
We use ILBs quite heavily and
Cloud Armor quite heavily,

00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:22.984
so we're looking forward
to some enhancements there.

00:49:22.984 --> 00:49:25.400
We're also looking forward to
some additional Interconnect

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:26.930
locations.

00:49:26.930 --> 00:49:29.000
And one of the big items
that we're looking for

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:30.770
is just some
general enhancements

00:49:30.770 --> 00:49:33.560
around VPC performance and
some of the managed services

00:49:33.560 --> 00:49:35.520
that might be announced
later this year.

00:49:35.520 --> 00:49:37.712
So with that, I'll
send it back on over.

00:49:37.712 --> 00:49:38.420
Thanks very much.

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:38.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:41.770
